# Choices and labels
@scenario tutorial_lesson_10
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code normal]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/10_choices_and_menus.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt routes]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Each choice has a display label and a destination. Pick a branch. Both branches print their own result and then return to the same line.
# region routes
> Run branch A -> branch_a
> Run branch B -> branch_b
@label branch_a
# endregion
[set tutorial_excerpt route_a]
# region route_a
[show ginger beside_code embarrassed]
ginger: {b}Branch A{/b} ran. Its final jump skips branch B and goes straight to the shared result.
[jump compare]
# endregion
@label branch_b
[set tutorial_excerpt route_b]
# region route_b
[show ginger beside_code confused]
ginger: {b}Branch B{/b} ran. The shared result follows immediately after this line.
@label compare
# endregion
narrator: {i}Shared result. Both routes have reached the same label.{/i}
[set tutorial_excerpt routes]
ginger: Find the arrows after the two choices. Follow each destination down the file. Only branch A needs an explicit jump to avoid displaying the other branch.
ginger: Change the visible text of branch A and leave its target alone. Run both options. Then swap the targets and compare the results again.
ginger: On a choice line, the text before the arrow is what the player reads. The identifier after it is the destination. Changing the wording leaves the destination intact.
ginger: After a branch has run, execution continues down the file unless a command redirects it. Branch A needs its jump because branch B sits between A and the shared result.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Trace a branching script -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: What does the arrow after a choice point to?
> A sprite expression. -> retry
> A destination label. -> correct
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: An expression selects artwork. Look for the named label further down the script.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: The arrow points to a label. The text the player sees can change without renaming that label.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_10]
[flag tutorial_done_10]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_1]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/10_choices_and_menus.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt routes]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: On paper, trace the labels from choosing A to the shared result. Repeat for B. Give each route a different mark so you can see where they first meet.
ginger: Change the visible wording of A but leave its target alone. Replay both routes and compare the result messages with your trace.
ginger: Now exchange only the two destinations. The menu wording stays familiar, but each choice runs the other branch. Restore the arrows before the next edit.
ginger: Temporarily remove A's jump to compare. Select A and count the branch messages. Selecting B is your control because B never relied on that jump.
@label practice_question
ginger: After removing A's jump, which selection can display both branch messages?
> Selecting B. -> practice_hint
> Selecting A. -> practice_result
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: B starts below A in the file. Follow the route that begins above both messages and look for the missing redirect.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: A now falls through into the B block before reaching compare. Restoring the jump makes A bypass B again.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
